    "articleEn": "Theme: Two scientists steer a space probe past an icy comet and fight to keep the communication signal alive.\n\nElena worked inside the command {{module}} of a huge {{spacecraft}}. Many crew members simply called it their home {{spaceship}}. They were currently on a three-year {{expedition}} across the solar system. Their main goal was peaceful space {{exploration}}. Today, Elena was in charge of a robotic {{probe}} that was about to complete a dangerous {{flyby}} near an unknown frozen comet.\n\nOn her screen, Elena observed the comet’s flight {{dynamics}} as it moved along a gentle {{curve}} around the sun. The probe fired its {{propulsion}} thrusters to stay on course. Suddenly, gas {{pressure}} began to build up near an emergency release {{vent}} at the {{tail}} of the machine. Elena could not speak because the radio channel was loud, so she turned to {{motion}} to her colleague, Leo, pointing at the red warning light.\n\nLeo quickly adjusted the controls. Under the comet’s icy, {{solid}} ground, the sensors detected a warm {{liquid}} ocean. This thick {{fluid}} moved in strange waves under the crust. Bright sunlight caused light {{refraction}} through the surface ice, creating a colorful light {{spectrum}} that shone with invisible {{ultraviolet}} rays.\n\n“Look at this sensor reading,” Leo whispered. The comet core contained strange {{radioactive}} minerals. Every tiny {{particle}} in the dust cloud held useful clues. The instruments showed how an {{atom}} could lose an {{electron}} and become a fast-moving {{ion}}. Soon, these combined into a new chemical {{molecule}}. A specialized {{quantum}} processor mapped this rapid rock {{formation}} in seconds.\n\nLeo wanted to test a new {{method}} to help the computer {{synthesis}} all the sensor readings into one complete file. However, an electrical {{circuit}} inside the probe suddenly overheated. The communication {{signal}} became weak, and its radio {{frequency}} began to jump wildly. Outside the ship, the long metal {{antenna}} failed to align with the probe.\n\nElena stayed calm. She adjusted the orientation across every spatial {{dimension}} until the beam locked back into place. The warning lights turned green, and the final data packets downloaded safely into the ship’s memory bank. Elena smiled, relieved that their daring journey had succeeded.",
    "articleZh": "主题： 两名科学家操控太空探测器飞越一颗冰冻彗星，并奋力维持通信信号畅通。\n\n埃莱娜在一艘庞大航天器的指令舱内工作。许多机组成员简单地称其为他们的家园宇宙飞船。他们目前正在进行一场穿越太阳系的为期三年的远征。他们的主要目标是和平的太空探索。今天，埃莱娜负责操控一个机械探测器，它即将完成对一颗未知冰冻彗星的危险飞越。\n\n在屏幕上，埃莱娜观察着彗星沿着绕日平缓曲线运动时的飞行动力学特性。探测器启动了推进推进器以保持航向。突然，机器尾部的一个紧急释放排气口附近开始积聚气体压力。由于无线电频道杂音很大，埃莱娜无法说话，于是她转身向同事利奥示意，指向那个红色的警告灯。\n\n利奥迅速调整了控制装置。在彗星冰冻的坚硬地面之下，传感器探测到了一片温暖的液体海洋。这种粘稠的流体在地壳下以奇怪的波浪形式涌动。耀眼的阳光穿过表面冰层引起光线折射，产生了一道闪烁着不可见紫外线的绚丽光谱。\n\n“看这个传感器读数，”利奥低声说道。彗星核心含有奇特的放射性矿物。尘埃云中的每一个微小粒子都包含着有用的线索。仪器展示了一个原子是如何失去一个电子并变成高速移动的离子的。很快，这些结合成了一种新的化学分子。一台专门的量子处理器在数秒内绘制出了这一快速岩石形成过程的图谱。\n\n利奥想测试一种新方法，以帮助计算机将所有传感器读数综合成一个完整的文件。然而，探测器内部的一个电路突然过热。通信信号变弱，其无线电频率开始剧烈跳动。飞船外部的长金属天线未能与探测器对准。\n\n埃莱娜保持着冷静。她在每一个空间维度上调整方向，直到波束重新锁定到位。警告灯变成了绿色，最后的数据包安全地下载到了飞船的存储库中。埃莱娜露出了微笑，为他们大胆的旅程取得成功而感到如释重负。",
