    "articleEn": "Theme: A student learns how to organize research and think carefully on paper.\n\nElena sat in the quiet library with a big stack of books. For her final university {{project}}, she had to write a major academic {{paper}}. Her professor told her that this work would prepare her for a master’s {{thesis}} or even a future doctoral {{dissertation}}. Earlier in the term, writing a short {{essay}} was simple, but this task felt much bigger. Right at the {{outset}}, she knew she needed a clear plan. She wrote a neat {{heading}} at the top of her clean page and began to make an {{outline}}.\n\nHer main goal was to {{introduce}} a new idea about student rest and study habits. First, she tried to catch the {{gist}} of several research articles. She wrote down each key {{point}} to see if it supported her own {{opinion}}. She read a short {{extract}} from a famous book and checked its {{abstract}} to see what the researchers found. Then she wrote a brief {{summary}} in her notebook. She wanted to {{cite}} every source properly, so she added each title to her {{reference}} list and made sure to {{quote}} only the most important sentences.\n\nElena knew a good researcher must not simply {{assume}} facts or {{presume}} that old books are always right. She wanted to test a fresh {{hypothesis}}. “I {{suppose}} quiet study rooms help people focus,” she whispered, “but some scientists {{postulate}} that light noise helps creative thinking.” She did not want to merely {{speculate}} or {{predict}} results without evidence. To gather real data, she prepared a survey to {{elicit}} honest answers from her classmates.\n\nAs she reviewed the survey results, she became {{conscious}} of subtle trends. It was hard to {{discern}} the full truth at first, but soon she could {{detect}} a pattern. She began to {{perceive}} how stress affected daily memory, and she could {{recognise}} where students felt tired. Many teachers {{reckon}} that more study hours mean better marks, and some {{deem}} late-night study necessary. But Elena’s findings seemed to {{imply}} something else. She took time to {{deliberate}} on these numbers, wondering how they could {{represent}} real student life.\n\nElena decided to {{persist}} even when the work became confusing. Her advisor used to {{insist}} that students must deeply {{understand}} their own data before writing. Elena tried hard to {{comprehend}} every detail. She began to {{analyse}} the survey numbers and tried to {{diagnose}} the mistakes in her early thinking. From the responses, she could {{infer}} why exam scores dropped and {{deduce}} that lack of sleep was the primary cause.\n\nTo explain her idea simply, she used an {{analogy}} about charging a mobile phone battery. She wanted to {{compare}} healthy resting habits with overworking. A clear {{contrast}} appeared between well-rested students and exhausted ones. Sometimes their study schedules would {{overlap}}, but Elena found a clear {{contradiction}} in the old beliefs: more hours did not always lead to better results. Finally, she was ready to {{conclude}} her study. She smiled, confident in her careful thinking.",
    "articleZh": "主题： 一名学生学会如何组织研究并在纸上缜密思考。\n\n埃莱娜坐在安静的图书馆里，身旁堆着厚厚的一叠书。作为大学的毕业项目，她必须写一篇重大的学术论文。导师曾告诉她，这项工作将为她日后撰写硕士论文甚至未来的博士学位论文打下基础。学期初写一篇短篇小论文还算轻松，但这扇任务显得艰巨得多。就在刚开始时，她便明白自己需要一个清晰的计划。她在干净的页面顶端工整地写下一个标题，并开始制定一份大纲。\n\n她的主要目标是引入关于学生休息与学习习惯的新观点。首先，她试图抓住几篇研究文章的主旨。她记下每一个核心要点，以观察它是否支持自己的观点。她阅读了一本名著的简短摘录，并查看了其摘要以了解研究人员的发现。接着，她在笔记本上写了一篇简短的总结。她希望能规范地引用每一个来源，因此将每个书名都添加到了自己的参考文献列表中，并确保只摘引最关键的句子。\n\n埃莱娜深知，一名优秀的研究者绝不能盲目假设事实，或直接假定古籍旧作永远正确。她想检验一个新的假设。“我料想安静的自习室有助于人们集中精力，”她低声自语道，“但一些科学家假定轻微的噪音有助于创造性思维。”她不想在毫无证据的情况下单纯去推测或预测结果。为了收集真实的数据，她准备了一份调查问卷，以引出同学们最真实的回答。\n\n在审阅调查结果时，她意识到了一些微妙的趋势。起初很难辨明全部的真相，但很快她就察觉出了一种规律。她开始感知压力是如何影响日常记忆的，并能辨认出学生们是在哪里感到疲倦的。许多老师认为学习时间越长成绩越好，有些人甚至视深夜学习为必要之举。但埃莱娜的调查结果似乎暗示着别的情况。她花时间仔细思忖这些数据，思考它们该如何代表真实的大学生活。\n\n即使工作变得令人困惑，埃莱娜也决定坚持下去。她的导师过去常常坚称，学生在动笔之前必须深刻理解自己的数据。埃莱娜竭尽全力去领悟每一个细节。她开始分析调查数据，并试图诊断出自己早期思维中的错误。从这些回答中，她能够推断出考试成绩下滑的原因，并推论出睡眠不足是罪魁祸首。\n\n为了通俗易懂地解释自己的想法，她用手机电池充电作了一个类比。她想将健康的休息习惯与过度劳累进行比较。在休息充足的学生与精疲力竭的学生之间，显现出了鲜明的对比。有时他们的学习作息会相互重叠，但埃莱娜在传统观念中发现了一个明显的矛盾：更长的时间并不总能带来更好的结果。最终，她准备好为自己的研究下结论了。她微笑着，对自己的缜密思考充满了信心。",
