    "articleEn": "Theme: Humans found clever ways to share thoughts using marks and sounds.\n\nMaya was preparing an exhibition at the city museum about human {{language}}. Long ago, before people wrote books, they shared thoughts with a friendly {{gesture}} or told an {{oral}} tale by the fire. On her table lay an ancient rope with a tight {{knot}}, once used to record numbers. Next to it was a flat stone {{sign}} pointing the way to water.\n\nShe looked at early written marks. One small rock showed a simple {{pictograph}} of a cow. Nearby was a piece of dry clay pressed with a sharp wooden {{wedge}}. A carved {{symbol}} could act as a {{logogram}} that stood for an entire object. Centuries later, humans simplified these pictures and created the first true {{alphabet}}. Maya wrote notes on these items, hoping the visitors would be able to read her quick {{handwriting}}.\n\nMaya was studying {{linguistics}} at university, so this project was very important to her. She wanted to show both written text and live {{verbal}} communication. Her professor walked into the room and handed her a set of headphones. They needed to test the listening station, which focused on the field of {{phonetics}}.\n\nMaya listened to a recorded {{utterance}} from an old speaker. She noticed how people {{pronounce}} words in different ways. The speaker used a regional {{dialect}}, and his strong {{accent}} gave the sentences a warm, deep feeling. When he asked a question, his {{intonation}} rose at the end, and a slight {{inflection}} in his pitch showed his surprise.\n\nMaya broke the audio track down into smaller parts. She listened to each single {{syllable}}. Within each beat, she found a soft {{vowel}} joined with a hard {{consonant}}. Changing just one tiny {{phoneme}} could transform a word into something completely different.\n\nFinally, Maya looked at how sentences were built. Good {{grammar}} helped people connect their thoughts, while clear {{syntax}} put the words in the right order. Together, these parts created {{semantic}} meaning that anyone could understand. Maya smiled, ready to guide the crowd through the long journey from clay marks to human speech.",
    "articleZh": "主题： 人类找到了利用符号和声音来分享思想的巧妙方法。\n\n玛雅正在城市博物馆筹备一场关于人类语言的展览。很久以前，在人们著书立作之前，他们通过友好的手势分享想法，或在炉火旁讲述口头故事。她的桌上放着一根打着紧实绳结的古老绳子，曾经用来记录数字。旁边是一块扁平的石头标记，指引着通往水源的方向。\n\n她看着早期的书写符号。一块小石头上画着一头奶牛的简单象形文字。附近有一块干黏土，上面压印着锋利的木制楔形物。雕刻出的符号可以作为代表整个物体的表意文字。几个世纪后，人类简化了这些图画，创造了第一批真正的字母表。玛雅为这些展品写下了注释，希望参观者能够看懂她匆匆写下的笔迹。\n\n玛雅在大学攻读语言学，因此这个项目对她来说非常重要。她希望同时展示书面文字和现场口头交流。她的教授走进房间，递给她一副耳机。他们需要测试专注于语音学领域的听力站。\n\n玛雅听了一位老人的录音话语。她注意到人们发音单词的方式各不相同。这位说话者使用的是地方方言，他浓重的口音让这些句子带有一种温暖而深沉的感觉。当他提出问题时，他的语调在末尾上扬，而音调的微妙语调变化流露出了他的惊讶。\n\n玛雅将音频轨道分解成更小的部分。她倾听每一个单独的音节。在每一个节拍中，她都能发现一个轻柔的元音与一个硬朗的辅音结合在一起。仅仅改变一个微小的音位，就能将一个词彻底变成完全不同的东西。\n\n最后，玛雅审视了句子的构成方式。良好的语法帮助人们连接思想，而清晰的句法则将词语置于正确的顺序。这些部分共同创造了任何人都能理解的语义含义。玛雅微笑着，准备带领人群走过从黏土印记到人类言语的漫长旅程。",
