    "articleEn": "Theme: A young editor works hard to organize words for a new book.\n\nElena sat at her wooden desk, surrounded by tall stacks of papers and old books. Her job this summer was to help {{compile}} a brand-new {{version}} of a school {{dictionary}}. The whole project was created to help international students improve their English {{vocabulary}}. However, the pile of unfinished cards felt very {{complicated}}, and the afternoon was passing quickly.\n\nToday, she had to label hundreds of words correctly. First, she checked each {{noun}} to see if it was {{singular}} or {{plural}}. Next, she examined every {{pronoun}} and marked how each {{verb}} changed in the past {{tense}}. Adding an {{adjective}} or an {{adverb}} was usually easy, but she had to pause whenever a {{preposition}} connected two ideas or a {{conjunction}} linked two complete thoughts. Her chief editor told her that every label must remain completely {{consistent}} across all pages.\n\nNext, Elena studied word building. She loved finding the historical {{root}} of an old term. Sometimes, a tiny {{prefix}} at the start totally changed a meaning, while a useful {{suffix}} at the end turned an action into an object. She also made sure each short {{abbreviation}} was clear so nobody would get confused. Right below the definitions, she carefully wrote a helpful {{synonym}} and an opposite {{antonym}} for each entry.\n\nBy four o’clock, the office grew warm and Elena began to feel tired. She still needed to review each special {{expression}}. She carefully separated a simple two-word {{phrase}} from a longer {{clause}} that had its own subject. Then she checked a colorful {{idiom}} that could not be understood from single words alone. The English language was wonderfully {{complex}}, but organizing it gave her deep satisfaction.\n\nHer manager, Mr. Davies, stopped by her desk and smiled warmly. He read through her freshly marked cards and nodded with great approval. Thanks to Elena’s patience and sharp eyes, the guide was finally ready to go to the printer.",
    "articleZh": "主题： 一位年轻编辑努力为一本新书整理词汇。\n\n埃琳娜坐在她的木制书桌前，周围堆满了高高的文件和旧书。她今年夏天的任务是协助编写一本全新版本的学校词典。整个项目旨在帮助国际学生提升他们的英语词汇量。然而，那一堆未处理的卡片显得非常复杂，而下午的时间正在飞快地溜走。\n\n今天，她必须正确标注成百上千个单词。首先，她检查每一个名词，看它是单数还是复数。接着，她审查每一个代词，并标注每个动词在过去时态中的变化。添加形容词或副词通常很简单，但每当遇到连接两个概念的介词，或是连接两个完整思想的连词时，她都不得不停下来仔细琢磨。她的主编告诉她，所有页面上的每一个标签都必须保持完全一致。\n\n随后，埃琳娜研究了构词法。她很喜欢寻找一个古老词汇的历史词根。有时，开头一个微小的前缀就能彻底改变词义，而末尾一个实用的后缀则能把一个动作变成一件物品。她还确保每一个简短的缩写都清晰明了，以免让人产生困惑。在释义正下方，她为每个词条细心地写下一个有用的同义词和一个相反的反义词。\n\n到了四点钟，办公室里变得有些闷热，埃琳娜也开始感到疲惫。她仍然需要复核每一个特殊的表达。她仔细地将一个简单的双词短语与带有自身主语的更长从句区分开来。然后，她核对了一个无法仅凭单个字面意思来理解的生动成语。英语是一门精妙复杂的语言，但整理它的过程给她带来了极大的满足感。\n\n她的主管戴维斯先生路过她的书桌，温和地微笑着。他翻阅了她刚刚标注好的卡片，赞许地点了点头。多亏了埃琳娜的耐心与敏锐的目光，这本指南终于可以送去付印了。",
